::ALG2_U03_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> - 16)/(x - 4). {
= x + 4, x &ne; 4#Correct. Factor as (x - 4)(x + 4), cancel the common factor, and keep x &ne; 4.
~ x - 4, x &ne; 4#The remaining factor is x + 4.
~ x + 4, no restriction#The original denominator still excludes x = 4.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 4, x &ne; 4#The numerator must be factored first.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>What restriction applies to 5/(x + 2)? {
~ x &ne; 2#The sign is reversed.
= x &ne; -2#Correct. The denominator x + 2 cannot equal 0.
~ x &ne; 5#The numerator does not create a restriction.
~ all real numbers#x = -2 makes the denominator 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> + 6x)/(x). {
~ x + 6, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 0.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 6#The common factor x should cancel.
= x + 6, x &ne; 0#Correct. Factor the numerator as x(x + 6) and keep the original restriction.
~ 6, x &ne; 0#Canceling x from only one term is not valid.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (3x/4)(8/x). {
~ 6x, x &ne; 0#The x cancels completely.
~ 2, x &ne; 0#The coefficient simplifies to 6.
~ 6, no restriction#The original denominator x excludes 0.
= 6, x &ne; 0#Correct. The x factors cancel and 24/4 = 6.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x + 5)/x by (x + 5)/3. {
= 3/x, x &ne; 0 and x &ne; -5#Correct. Multiply by 3/(x + 5); x + 5 cancels but the divisor cannot be 0.
~ x/3#This does not multiply by the reciprocal correctly.
~ 3/x, x &ne; 0#The divisor also creates x &ne; -5.
~ 3#The x remains in the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> - 25)/(x - 5). {
~ x - 5, x &ne; 5#The remaining factor is x + 5.
= x + 5, x &ne; 5#Correct. Use difference of squares and keep the original denominator restriction.
~ x + 5, x &ne; -5#The denominator x - 5 excludes 5.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 5, x &ne; 5#The numerator must be factored.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>What values are excluded from (x + 1)/((x - 2)(x + 3))? {
~ x &ne; -1#The numerator does not create restrictions.
~ x &ne; -2 and x &ne; 3#Both signs are reversed.
= x &ne; 2 and x &ne; -3#Correct. Each denominator factor cannot equal 0.
~ all real numbers#The denominator is zero at 2 and -3.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (4x<sup>3</sup>)/(8x). {
~ 2x<sup>2</sup>, x &ne; 0#The coefficient should be 1/2, not 2.
~ x<sup>3</sup>/2, x &ne; 0#One x factor cancels.
~ x<sup>2</sup>/2, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 0.
= x<sup>2</sup>/2, x &ne; 0#Correct. Reduce 4/8 to 1/2 and cancel one factor of x.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (x - 1)/(x + 2) by (x + 2)/(x + 5). {
= (x - 1)/(x + 5), x &ne; -2 and x &ne; -5#Correct. Cancel x + 2 and keep restrictions from original denominators.
~ (x - 1)/(x + 2), x &ne; -2#The x + 2 factor cancels.
~ (x - 1)/(x + 5), no restriction#Original denominators create restrictions.
~ (x + 2)/(x + 5)#The wrong factor remains.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why can (x + 4)/x not simplify to 4? {
~ because x is never allowed in a denominator#x can be in a denominator with restrictions.
= x is not a factor of every numerator term#Correct. Only common factors can cancel across a fraction.
~ because 4 is not a polynomial#4 is a polynomial constant.
~ because addition always cancels first#Terms do not cancel like factors.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> - x)/(x - 1). {
~ x - 1, x &ne; 1#The remaining factor is x.
~ x, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 1.
= x, x &ne; 1#Correct. Factor x(x - 1), cancel x - 1, and keep x &ne; 1.
~ 1, x &ne; 1#The factor x remains.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>What restriction applies to (2x)/(x<sup>2</sup> - 9)? {
~ x &ne; 9#The denominator zeros are not 9.
~ x &ne; -9#This is not a zero of x<sup>2</sup> - 9.
~ x &ne; 0#The numerator does not create a denominator restriction.
= x &ne; 3 and x &ne; -3#Correct. Factor the denominator as (x - 3)(x + 3).
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (9x)/(3x<sup>2</sup>). {
= 3/x, x &ne; 0#Correct. Reduce 9/3 to 3 and cancel one x.
~ 3x, x &ne; 0#The remaining x is in the denominator.
~ 6/x, x &ne; 0#The coefficients divide to 3.
~ 3/x, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Divide (x - 3)/(x + 4) by (x - 3)/(x - 1). {
~ (x + 4)/(x - 1)#This flips the final result.
= (x - 1)/(x + 4), x &ne; -4, 1, and 3#Correct. Multiply by the reciprocal and keep restrictions from the original denominator and divisor.
~ (x - 1)/(x + 4), x &ne; -4#The divisor also excludes x = 1 and x = 3.
~ 1#Only one common factor cancels.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> + 3x + 2)/(x + 1). {
~ x + 1, x &ne; -1#The remaining factor is x + 2.
~ x + 2, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = -1.
= x + 2, x &ne; -1#Correct. Factor the numerator as (x + 1)(x + 2).
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 2, x &ne; -1#The numerator must be factored.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>What restriction applies to (x - 8)/(4x)? {
~ x &ne; 8#The numerator does not create a restriction.
~ x &ne; 4#The coefficient 4 does not make the denominator zero.
~ all real numbers#x = 0 makes the denominator 0.
= x &ne; 0#Correct. The denominator 4x is zero when x = 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (12x<sup>2</sup>y)/(3xy), assuming variables in denominators are nonzero. {
= 4x#Correct. Divide coefficients and cancel one x and one y.
~ 4xy#The y cancels completely.
~ 9x#The coefficients divide to 4.
~ 4/y#The remaining factor is x, not 1/y.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (x/(x - 2))((x - 2)/(x + 1)). {
~ x/(x - 2), x &ne; 2#The x - 2 factor cancels.
= x/(x + 1), x &ne; 2 and x &ne; -1#Correct. Cancel x - 2 and keep restrictions from original denominators.
~ x/(x + 1), no restriction#Original denominators create restrictions.
~ 1/(x + 1)#The numerator x remains.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is undefined at x = 6? {
~ (x - 6)/4#The denominator is 4, not zero.
~ x + 6#This polynomial is defined for all real x.
= 4/(x - 6)#Correct. The denominator is zero when x = 6.
~ 6/x#This is undefined at x = 0, not x = 6.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> - 4)/(x<sup>2</sup> + 3x + 2). {
~ (x + 2)/(x + 1), x &ne; -1 and x &ne; -2#The remaining numerator factor is x - 2.
~ (x - 2)/(x + 1), x &ne; -1#The original denominator also excludes x = -2.
~ x - 2, x &ne; -2#The denominator factor x + 1 remains.
= (x - 2)/(x + 1), x &ne; -1 and x &ne; -2#Correct. Factor numerator and denominator, then cancel x + 2.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is the reciprocal of (x - 5)/(x + 2)? {
= (x + 2)/(x - 5)#Correct. The reciprocal flips numerator and denominator.
~ (x - 5)/(x + 2)#This is the original expression.
~ -(x - 5)/(x + 2)#A reciprocal is not a sign change.
~ (x + 5)/(x - 2)#Signs inside the factors do not change.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> - 1)/(x + 1). {
~ x + 1, x &ne; -1#The remaining factor is x - 1.
= x - 1, x &ne; -1#Correct. Factor as (x - 1)(x + 1), cancel, and keep the restriction.
~ x - 1, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = -1.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 1, x &ne; -1#Use difference of squares.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 15)/(x + 3). {
~ x + 3, x &ne; -3#The remaining factor is x + 5.
~ x + 5, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = -3.
= x + 5, x &ne; -3#Correct. Factor the numerator as (x + 3)(x + 5), cancel, and keep x &ne; -3.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 5, x &ne; -3#The numerator must be factored.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Multiply (5/(x - 1))((x - 1)/(2x)). {
~ 5/2, x &ne; 1#The x remains in the denominator.
~ 5/(2x), no restriction#Original denominators create restrictions.
~ 2x/5#This flips the product.
= 5/(2x), x &ne; 1 and x &ne; 0#Correct. Cancel x - 1 and keep restrictions from both original denominators.
}

::ALG2_U03_L04_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.6<br><br>Question:<br>Divide x/(x + 6) by 3/(x + 6). {
= x/3, x &ne; -6#Correct. Multiply by the reciprocal (x + 6)/3 and cancel x + 6.
~ 3/x, x &ne; -6#This is the reciprocal of the simplified result.
~ x/3, no restriction#The original denominator excludes x = -6.
~ 1/3, x &ne; -6#The numerator x remains.
}

